# @PROJECT_NAME@

What @PROJECT_NAME@ does, in a sentence or two.

## Building

Requirements: LLVM 23 (clang, clang-tidy, clang-format), CMake 3.29 or
later, Ninja, and vcpkg with `VCPKG_ROOT` set to its folder. The cpp-policy
README lists them ("Requirements") and says how to install LLVM and Ninja
("Setup per platform").
Dependencies come from vcpkg (`vcpkg.json`, pinned by its
`builtin-baseline`).

```
sh tools/hooks/gate                    # the local gate: audit, format, build with clang-tidy, tests
sh tools/hooks/gate release            # optimized build, size budget, benchmarks
cmake --workflow --preset unit         # quick: no clang-tidy, optimized, unit tests only
```

On Windows the presets carry a `win-` prefix (`win-check`, `win-unit`,
`win-release`); `tools/hooks/gate` adds it itself.

Enable the git hooks once per clone:

```
git config core.hooksPath tools/hooks
```

`pre-commit` runs the audit, the format check and clang-tidy on the staged
C++ files; `commit-msg` checks the message (Conventional Commits with a body
that says why); `pre-push` runs the local gate. After editing C++
files, `sh tools/hooks/tidy-files <file>...` checks just those files.

This project follows [cpp-policy](https://github.com/luiserh1/cpp-policy),
pinned in `CMakeLists.txt` (@POLICY_TAG@). `.clang-tidy`, `.clang-format`,
`CMakePresets.json` and `tools/hooks/` are its copies and must not be edited
here; upgrade with its `tools/upgrade.sh`.

Every push runs the CI gate, cpp-policy's `gate.yml`, in GitHub Actions
(`.github/workflows/ci.yml`).

## Layout

`src/` is split into layers, from the lowest up. Code may include headers
from its own layer or a lower one:

| Layer | Modules | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| 1 (bottom) | `src/lowlevel/` | Confined OS code |
| 2 | `src/app/` | The program's logic |
| 3 (top) | `main.cpp` | Program boundary |

`tests/` has one doctest program per kind of test and module
(`cpp_policy_add_tests()` in `tests/CMakeLists.txt`). A module's unit tests
are one CTest test, `unit/<module>/all test cases`; each integration or
benchmark test case is its own, `<kind>/<module>/<test case>`.
`ctest --preset check -L unit` runs the unit tests; one unit test case runs
by hand with `<program> --test-case="<name>"`. A `-R` that matches nothing
fails.
